{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeryE8dc8dxDuSEcf842X","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done.\n\n## Files changed\n\n| File | Change |\n|---|---|\n| `experiments/tidal-poster/index.html` | Aweigh `.project-media` markup rebuilt as a `.device-pair`; swapped `../living-poster/page.js` for local `devices.js` |\n| `experiments/tidal-poster/tide.css` | Appended a scoped `.ferry-media …` block (all rules prefixed, nothing else touched) |\n| `experiments/tidal-poster/devices.js` | New, sole owner of watch playback state |\n| `tmp/opus-tidal-devices-report.md` | Reference notes with the measurements and fit tables |\n\nNothing else was edited. `poster.css`, `page.js`, `scene.js`, `arrival.js`, the cloud/material/weather/tree/ferry files, and `aweigh/` are untouched (read-only inspection only).\n\n## The actual bug\n\n`poster.css` had `.watch video { border-radius: 24%; object-fit: fill }`. A percentage radius resolves per axis, so on a 74.464%×56.477% box it produced ellipses much rounder than the frame's cutout — that's the map art bleeding through at the screen corners. `object-fit: fill` also stretched the 416×496 recording, and there was no masking element or opaque backing between the video and the well. `portfolio.css:844` documents the identical trap for the production hero watch.\n\n## Asset choices\n\n- **Watch frame** `watchos-frame.webp` (1120×1760, alpha), aperture 834×994 at y=384 → `top 21.818% / left 12.768% / 74.464% / 56.477%`, straight from `aweigh/style.css`.\n- **Phone frame** `ios-frame.webp` (1380×2880, alpha) replaces the faked `7px` CSS border. Aperture `top 2.535% / left 4.348% / 91.304% / 95%`, radius `13% / 6%` per the Aweigh site.\n- **Screenshot** stayed `ios-delay-tracking.webp` — it is 1260×2736, exactly the aperture, so `cover` crops nothing.\n- **Video** moved to `watchos-loop.hevc.mp4` / `.mp4`. Its crossfade is baked in (see `portfolio.html:100`), so one native `loop` element gives the smooth loop that the Aweigh hero needs a two-video double-buffer and a 3s hold to fake. Poster `watchos-poster.webp` matches the aperture ratio.\n\n## Checks run\n\n- `node --check devices.js` passes; CSS braces balanced (107/107); HTML tag nesting parses with no unclosed or mismatched tags.\n- Watch video 0.83871 vs aperture 0.83903 → 0.039% vertical overflow, so no letterbox and no stretch.\n- Corner radius `15.44% / 12.96%` resolves to 0.11497 and 0.11502 watch-widths — equal pixels, circular corners, matching the `11.5cqw` value `portfolio.css` landed on, without needing container queries.\n- Pair width vs. available width computed at 1440/1100/760/600/390/320px: fits at all six, worst case 150px of 250px. Pair proportions follow the Aweigh `device-pair` ratio (0.4011 vs its 0.4016).\n- Play control clearance: the pair's floor sits 78px (62px on mobile) above the well bottom; the button's top edge is ~66px (~50px). No overlap at any width.\n- Frame alpha confirmed on both webps by reading the VP8X flag byte.\n\n## Playback behaviour\n\n`page.js` was the only script bound to `#watch-video` and did nothing else here, so replacing it keeps a single owner. Muted, `playsinline`, `preload=\"none\"` until first needed; starts at ≥35% visibility, pauses offscreen and on `visibilitychange`; a deliberate pause sticks and is never auto-resumed. Reduced motion starts paused and clears `loop`, so a requested play runs once then offers \"Replay watch demo\". Rejected autoplay is silent (it's expected); a rejected press writes to the `role=\"status\"` line. The button ships `hidden` and is revealed by JS, matching `portfolio.html`.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- No browser QA from me, as instructed — the numbers above are computed, not observed. The compositional judgment worth your eye: the pair is now fully visible and foot-aligned rather than bleeding off the bottom, which is calmer but less dramatic than before.\n- The well is `aspect-ratio: 1.8` from shared `poster.css`, which is wide for a fully visible pair, so there's a lot of map on either side. Narrowing the well would size the devices up, but it lives in shared CSS and changes the page rhythm, so I left it. Noted in the report.\n- I did not port the Aweigh `watch-loader` spinner; the poster frame covers the buffering window. Also noted."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":924,"cache_read_input_tokens":85201,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":924},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fd659ea1-d238-4b16-bb34-5e9a13c92fc0","uuid":"91802739-5b01-495d-b70e-92a235af67f6","timestamp":"2026-09-09T01:07:45.725Z","request_id":"req_011CeryE7pVYGwoUAyEgmvSF"}
